the green knight is adapted from a 14th  century epic poem called sir gawain and  the green knight  this is a sampling of some of the  biggest questions the film probably left  you with  some that can be answered by  investigating the original text and some  meant to be unanswerable  when gawain heads off to celebrate  christmas with a king and queen his  mother opts to stay home  she says she isn't feeling up to  partying but she's very up to casting  some spells  she and three other women perform a  ritual which ultimately summons the  green knight  and you would be absolutely right to ask  why the heck she'd do that  after all if not for this summoning  gwayne would never have ended up in the  situation he finds himself in  the green knight never answers this  question but sir wayne in the green  knight does  at least kind of the thing to remember  here is who the players are  the king and queen for example aren't  just anyone they are king arthur and  queen guinevere  and gwen's mother is arthur's sister  making her morgan le faye  an enchantress and not exactly a great  sibling in the original text lepay's  intention is to frighten gwenevere and  also prove that arthur's knights aren't  as heroic as they're made out to be  whether these motors remain the same in  the green knights is unclear  one would think she might be upset that  her son winds up connected with the  green knight in such a lethal way but  she seems fine with it  perhaps she resents her son or perhaps  she believes the quest gawain must go on  thanks to the green knight will lead him  to succeeding arthur as king  either way we're never supplied with a  definitive answer  shortly into gawain's quest to find the  green chapel he comes upon a young man  hovering over the dead bodies from a  recent battle  the young man seems like he wants to  help gwen but he turns out to be a thief  both the thief and his two compatriots  leave gawain tied up while stealing both  his horse and his axe the one he got  from the green knight at the beginning  of the tale  after escaping his bonds gwen comes upon  a small cottage haunted by the ghost of  its former resident  winifred played by aaron kellyman  winifred reveals that someone chopped  her head off and dumped it in the nearby  lake  she even asked wayne if he was the one  who killed her gwayne decides to help  win it fred by fetching her head and  returning it to the rest of her dead  body in the cottage  when he does winifred reveals that the  green knight to someone gawain knows and  gwen's axe magically appears  where did the axe come from did the  thief who stole the axe kill winifred  that seems unlikely since winifred is  already decayed to a full skeleton  however time seems to operate very  strangely in the green knight so it is  possible  regardless we never find out for sure  who killed winifred or how gawain's axe  finds its way into her cottage other  than to say that maybe gwayne isn't  quite all there  speaking of which gwen spends much of  his journey without any human  companionship  as we've seen even when he does  encounter other people things don't work  out terribly well for him  beaten down by the elements and starving  he winds up eating some wild mushrooms  moments later he vomits and begins to  imagine moss growing all over his hands  although the movie never states this  explicitly it's a safe bet that those  mushrooms were a little poisonous  and a lot hallucinogenic the real  question is how long do those  hallucinations last  furthermore how reliable a narrator is  gawain even from the start  while tied up by thebes he seems to  imagine himself as a rotting skeleton  that never escaped its bonds  that alone makes it difficult to know  what's real and what isn't along wayne's  quest  and things get even more unbelievable  when gwen runs into a group of naked  giants roaming the countryside  he attempts to get them to carry him the  rest of the way to the green chapel  instead they're warded off by a fox who  spends much of the film following and  perhaps  guiding gwen on his journey are the  giants real  the green knight never answers this  question the fact that he's never aided  by the giants suggests that they're  probably just a hallucination  in fact since gawain feeds to fox some  of those wild mushrooms it's possible  they're both hallucinating  assuming the fox is real of course  weaving in and out of gwen's journey to  the green chapel is a fox who seems to  act as companion and guide  they rest in a cave together they eat  those mushrooms together and by the end  of the story the fox even speaks to  queen cautioning him away from meeting  with the green knight  is the fox friend or foe and what does  the fox represent in the larger  narrative of the green knight  boxes hold all sorts of different  symbolic meanings across history and  cultures  they can represent that a solution is at  hand or they can represent a need for  patience  sometimes they're benevolent sometimes  they're tricksters meant to be eyed  warily  in the context of sir gawain and the  green knight the fox is tied to a  specific character the wife of a lord  gwain encounters late in his travels  in the original text gwen stays with the  pair in their castle and forms a pact  with the lord the lord will provide  guane with the best food he captures in  his hunt  and in exchange gwen will give the lord  anything he gains during his stay in the  castle during this time  the lord's wife kisses gwen two times  before giving him a magical protective  green belt  the lord struggles to succeed in his  hunt that day he is of course hunting a  fox  the fox is meant to possess a similar  guile and manipulative nature as the  lord's wife does attempting to bed  gawain  in the green night the lord captures the  fox only to let it get away after gwen  refuses to give up the belt  in other words we're left open-ended  over whether or not the fox can be  trusted in the movie  the lord and his wife are not the only  two people gwen meets during his time in  the lord's castle  there is a third ever present figure  throughout this part of the film the  gray-haired perpetually blindfolded  sightless woman  she appears during meals and more  importantly she's present when the  lord's wife eventually succeeds in  seducing gwen with a green  belt who is the sightless woman and why  is she there  at no point does the green knight  directly answer this question however in  the poem sir gawain in the green knight  she's morgan le faye  and that's relevant to the film as well  at the start of his journey gawain is  given what appears to be the same green  belt by his mother  however when gowan is captured by  thieves they steal the belt along with  most of his other belongings  he is completely shocked when the lord's  wife somehow offers what seems to be the  same green belt later  one could hypothesize that it is the  same green belt and that it comes  courtesy of the sightless woman who is  actually gwen's mother in disguise  we're never told that explicitly but  that is one possible interpretation  after all both the lord's wife and  gwen's paramour back home essel are  portrayed by alicia vikander so why  couldn't his mother and the sightless  woman secretly be the same person  gawain has one ultimate end point for  his journey to meet the green knights in  the green chapel on christmas day a year  after their first encounter  we don't know why his mother conjured  the night and we also don't know who the  green knight is  beyond what appears to be a supernatural  being incapable of death  however during gwen's journey winfred  tells him the green knight to someone he  knows  later the fox wards gawain away from the  green knight explaining that the figure  is inhuman and utterly without  mercy so who exactly is the green knight  the movie never actually explains this  however in the poem sir wayne and the  green knights were told definitively who  he is  he's the lord from the castle entreated  and enchanted by morgan le faye as a  means to bring harm to arthur and  camelot  the film adaptation gives us no such  connection between the lord and the  green knight  it's possible that they're the same  person but it's more likely that in this  version the green knight isn't so much a  someone green nose but a some thing  the green knight represents the horrible  knowledge that gawain would make a bad  night a bad father and a tragically  unfit king  the green knight brings gwen this  knowledge through a merciless vision so  horrifying that gwen chooses to be  decapitated by the green knights rather  than live  do we know who the green knight really  is no but we do know what he is  wayne's mortal fate check out one of our  newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

